WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:07.339
This book is different from other corporate
governance books

00:00:07.339 --> 00:00:11.360
on the market, in that it's really evidence
driven, the way

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.200
we structure the book is around facts and
evidence.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.150
So what we do with each section is we start
with an idea.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.039
We explain how it works, and what the positives
and

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:25.910
negatives are of different approaches to governance.
And then we look at the data on how prevalent

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:28.019
certain things
are out there in the market.

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:31.119
And we follow it up with academic research
which tests

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:35.000
whether these things are positive or negative.
And we draw our conclusions from that.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.530
A lot of the books that are on the market
today are really

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.820
opinion-based.
So they start with a central premise, and

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:43.350
then the author
spends the majority of the book defending

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.280
why that premise
is correct.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.329
A lot of it is based on that person's opinions,
and, in some

00:00:50.329 --> 00:00:53.340
cases, biases.
But they're not really evidence driven, and

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.629
in many cases,
those conclusions may or may not be true.

00:00:56.629 --> 00:01:05.519
But they're not really tested.
I think the most surprising finding from the

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:08.970
book is that a
lot of the focus is misdirected in governance

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.770
today.
A lot of the attention is placed on features.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:14.920
So, for example, what's the structure of the
board of

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.660
directors, how much compensation is paid to
CEOs and things

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.210
like that and a lot less attention is paid
on the factors

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.750
that really matter.
And these are the processes by which the company

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.090
actually
makes decisions.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:31.080
So, for example a lot less attention is played,
is paid to

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.470
the strategy and risk management functions
and those are

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:39.490
areas where boards can really stand to improve.
Another area where boards could stand to improve

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.860
is with its
CEO succession.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.430
A lot of CEO succession planning within corporations
is, was

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.240
shown to be a more check the box solution
and not really

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.490
what we expected.
We expected that companies would have operations

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.490
plans in
place.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.490
Things that they could do if the CEO suddenly
stepped down

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:01.291
and they needed to find somebody right away
and I think

00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:05.540
going forward a lot can improve with a new
focus on the

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.789
features and of how governance are run rather
than just the

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:13.940
superficial factors.
I think the most surprising finding of the

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:17.130
research is that,
how little impact the research has actually

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:22.270
had on practice,
there's tons of academic research across accounting,

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:26.769
finance, economics, corporate strategy and
things like that,

00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:29.799
lots of careful thought but I don't think
that research has

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:33.799
had that much impact in a way people actually
think about

00:02:33.799 --> 00:02:35.790
it.
You know, the Blue Ribbon Panels that are

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.860
filled with
clearly well known people, very smart, very

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.770
successful and
you read the reports there�s actually not

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:45.579
much discussion of
the research that either supports or doesn't

00:02:45.579 --> 00:02:46.820
support some of
the findings.

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:51.290
I think one of things that we hope the book
does is provides

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.820
a synthesis of the research in a digestible
form that the

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:59.949
press, directors, lawyers, government regulators
and alike

00:02:59.949 --> 00:03:03.660
can actually see that there is a body of work
out there that

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.090
informs and you don't have to do it yourself,
it's already

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:11.840
being done as part of the scholarship and
universities so

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.940
we're actually a little surprised how little
impact it's

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:20.799
sort of had in these things.
Other kind of more practical things that,

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:24.940
that we found
surprising are the inability of boards to

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:30.150
actually put in,
put in operational succession plans, this

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.620
is a, a really a
key part of risk management, and it's, it's

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.030
a pretty, it's a
pretty serious part of, of what boards do.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:42.311
I mean, ultimately they evaluate and invent
the strategy of

00:03:42.311 --> 00:03:44.040
the organization and they kind of hire and
fire the top

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.570
management.
And, and so, you know, one of the two issues

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:54.579
seems to be
lacking a little bit.

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:57.340
The boards are struggling with, with lots
of things with

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:01.180
regard to CEO pay.
Now that's not surprising in and of itself,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:05.949
but, the SEC has
recently, required boards to talk about, are

00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:11.430
the pay
practices motivating managers to do excessive

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.890
risk taking.
That sounds like something like board will

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.290
evaluate.
But I think we don't really know at this point

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.540
how do you
define excessive risk taking you know, what

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:24.720
would it look
like you know and then the issue is, you know,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.521
how can you
evaluate that and so, we actually have some

00:04:27.521 --> 00:04:32.370
proposals, you
know sort of hopefully informed guesses about

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.910
new ways to
think about pays, how much incentive are you

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:38.830
really
providing and when you are in a situation

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.500
you start worrying
about excessive risk taking.

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.690
One of the examples that we have in the, in
the book has to

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:48.950
do with Eli Lily and they, they went through
and did a very

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:52.910
detailed assessment of, of excessive risk
taking.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:57.210
And there response was to basically take out
all the, lots

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.450
of the incentive contracts.
They got rid of stock options.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:05.420
The capped of bonuses and things like that.
Now, that may be fine for Eli Lilly but you

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.480
kind a wonder
about what happens if a lot of companies did

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:11.130
that.
I mean if the incentives that actually drive

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.700
the innovation
that really motivates people.

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:17.290
And I think taking these things out is not
necessarily a

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.370
good result for sure, to mitigate risk, but
substantially

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:32.210
reduce the returns for companies as well.
We employed a very rigorous and methodical

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.950
methodology and
it took a long time to do that.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:38.120
I mean, this book was almost 3 years in the
making and with

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:42.340
each feature in governance we went through
and collected all

00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:47.310
the literature both from academics and professionals.
We looked at the data that was available and

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.240
we looked
through the popular press to find examples

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.400
of things that
companies are specifically doing in these

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.580
areas.
And we also added interviews that we conducted

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.680
with third
party experts, consultants, lawyers, and practitioners

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.210
including directors and executives.
And we took all that information and digested

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:11.400
it together in
a way that, we believe, gives the most comprehensive

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:19.050
look
at, all areas of governance.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:23.230
In the media shareholder discussions and things
like that,

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.500
there's, I think there's lots of things that
are, that are

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:27.860
missing.
First off, I think people are trying to do

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.071
the right thing,
you know.

00:06:29.071 --> 00:06:33.790
You want to highlight and penalize people
where they've had

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:36.350
bad governance, and they're doing kind of
bad things.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:40.210
I think that, that's a good thing.
The issue is what mechanism do you want to

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.250
use to isolate
the ones that are the, the good ones from

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:47.840
the bad ones?
I think what's missing is everybody, or most

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.700
people out
there are, are really trying to find, here's

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:54.230
a very, very
simple yard stick for measuring corporate

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:57.970
governments.
Do you have this many directors of this type

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:01.139
or is the pay
of this form?

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:04.139
Things like that, but I think the problem
is it's a lot more

00:07:04.139 --> 00:07:06.930
complicated than that, there are certain differences
in

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:11.350
organizations and people, corporate strategies,
things like

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:16.510
that, that basically make a simple yard stick
a place to

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:20.160
start but not a place to end up.
You have to think more carefully about what

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:26.040
are the choices
and it's not a one size fits all set of choices

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:33.870
for
evaluating corporate governance.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.840
Boards faced lots sensitive issues.
And, and the issues that are really sensitive

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:39.930
are varied
over time.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:45.720
In the, 1990s, 2000, early 2000s you know,
we had lots of

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:50.490
audit failures.
The board was very concerned about, are, is

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:51.800
the audit
function appropriate?

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:55.560
Do we have sufficient internal controls?
The audit committee of the board was under

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:56.639
tremendous
scrutiny.

00:07:56.639 --> 00:08:00.280
I think now what's, what you see is that the
Comps H.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:03.940
Committee is the, is the committee and the
decisions that

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.720
they're making are the ones under tremendous
scrutiny.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:10.040
Is CEO pay too high, are we paying the people
the right form

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:13.410
and there's lots of people on the outside
both media but

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:16.780
also proxy advisory firms that are weighting
in on this and

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:19.980
it's actually now is going to share holder
vote through the

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:23.910
so called say on pay.
So, I'd say that's a tremendous sensitive

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.110
issue for the
boards.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:29.410
The other issues that are sensitive are risk
management.

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:32.389
Do we actually know what's going to happen
if we have some

00:08:32.389 --> 00:08:37.469
sort of hiccup in the, the inputs or the outputs
of the

00:08:37.469 --> 00:08:41.690
firm?
You know if we have an earthquake in Japan,

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:44.040
how, how's it
effect where their going to be able to get

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:45.040
our raw
materials.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.360
Do we have plans in place and things like
that?

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.080
What happens to us, if all of a sudden interest
rates go up

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.300
or we lose a major customer, you know, what
is our strategy

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:59.390
capable of dealing with that?
So lots of boards are trying to deal with

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:03.300
risk management
and one key part of it that we've seen certainly

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.480
around the
country recently is succession planning.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:09.042
Do they actually have somebody that can plug,
that can plug

00:09:09.042 --> 00:09:14.580
in, do we know who that person is.
How comfortable do we feel about that as we?

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.040
Yeah we place management.
I think what's going to happen going forward

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.970
is I think the
next committee of the board is the nominating

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:23.970
governors
committee.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:26.790
There's tremendous scrutiny now about if we
have sufficient

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.300
diversity on board, do we have the right board
members.

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:33.210
You know the people had come up for evaluation
for new board

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:37.370
memberships.
We actually come up through the govern, the

00:09:37.370 --> 00:09:39.200
nominating
governance committee.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.040
So how that committee operates, are they doing
the right

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:43.620
thing?
Are they picking the right people?

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.101
Those kind of things, I think, are, are clearly
on the

00:09:45.101 --> 00:09:54.490
horizon.
The book has lots of important takeaways.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:57.010
One of them is that I think you wanna be very
cautious

00:09:57.010 --> 00:10:01.850
following blue ribbon panel recommendations,
or, you know,

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:06.149
things that you read in the press.
Or what are perceived to be best practices.

00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:08.500
And when we look at these things in the book
from a research

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.920
perspective, a lot of these things just don't
hold up, some

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:17.990
of them are clearly fine, some of them, not.
So I think the book really lays it out in

00:10:17.990 --> 00:10:19.290
a pretty neutral
way.

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:22.300
And gets in there and says, what do we actually
know about

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:25.380
things like, should the CEO and chairman of
the board be

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.530
separated?
You know, what do we know about how you should

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:30.810
structure
pay, and things like that?

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:33.460
But I think the, the primary message of the
book is you have

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.500
to think a little harder about governance
choices.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:39.620
You just can't follow these, these standard
recommendations.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:47.510
Yeah in terms of pressing the developments,
the thing we

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:51.100
worry about is for lack of a better term,
the federalization

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:55.941
of corporate governments.
We've certainly seen Starbaines Axially go

00:10:55.941 --> 00:10:58.850
into place, it
was directed mostly at the accounting function

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:02.390
and other
features of the board but more recently the

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:07.080
Dodge Frank
bill, which has been passed in some form although

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:11.830
is being
litigated, really opens up the board to lots

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:16.150
of inquiries
that are basically imposed on them by the

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:19.190
government.
So one of the things is they're opening up

00:11:19.190 --> 00:11:23.150
a proxy access on
the rubric of shareholder democracy.

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:26.080
So if, if you've, you're a shareholder you
oughta be able to

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.770
vote, you know, in America an, and have some
impact on these

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:32.980
things with regard to directors that are elected
and things

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:39.760
like that, one of the proposals is if you
own 3% you or a, a

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:44.490
group have collectively owned 3% of the company
for 3 years

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:50.430
you can nominate 25% of the board for, for
elections.

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:54.500
So instead of having elections which are mostly
uncontested

00:11:54.500 --> 00:12:00.420
except in proxy contests you have a situation
here where the

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:05.180
outsiders can bring board members to forward
for potential

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:08.740
election to the board.
Now, that's something that's being imposed

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:11.160
on our companies,
that's not something that they would do necessarily

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.300
of their
free will, and you kind of wonder is that

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:19.310
a, is that a good
consequence or not, basically, we've actually

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:22.080
done a little
research around this and we find that those

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:24.560
kind of
proposals seem to be greeted with big negative

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:27.830
stock price
responses for companies that look like they're

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:29.900
going to be
subject to these, to these criteria.

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:34.150
So there's a case where you know, the government
has, in

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:37.320
some sense, decided, that's a good idea, have
kinda passed

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:41.880
this thing.
But it's cert-, the research results are suggesting

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:43.430
that
maybe it's not such a good idea.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:47.200
So hopefully, you know, the research like
that will inform,

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.990
regulators in the government, you know, what
might be a good

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:53.670
thing to do.
But I think, right now, in the administration,

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:57.459
there's a lot
of interest in, you know, imposing.

00:12:57.459 --> 00:13:02.779
Governance criteria on company that really
could have been

00:13:02.779 --> 00:13:06.830
voluntarily adopted and weren't.
And the, you know, the, I don't know, the

00:13:06.830 --> 00:13:11.070
trillion dollar
question is, is that good or not?

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:15.070
Is it better to have regulation or is it better
to let the

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:17.839
market operate?
Of course, that's the age old question in

00:13:17.839 --> 00:13:20.660
economics.
And I think here's a case where I think we

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:22.760
can weigh in a
little bit on this.

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:26.210
But I think the federalization of corporate
governance is a

00:13:26.210 --> 00:13:26.399
big.

